        target(":server-lib"),
    ]),
)

# ── Deploy boundary (Phase 4 — snowydeer Nix→Buck2→Nix sandwich) ─────────────
#
# nudox_pkg is the snowydeer-packaged release binary used by the nixos companion
# repo (Colmena) to import the server into the Nix store.
#
# Static-linking decision:
#   The server dep graph has NO llvm-sys or system openssl dependency:
#     - libsqlite3_sys uses the "bundled" feature (statically compiled C SQLite).
#     - reqwest/hyper use rustls features (pure-Rust TLS, no libssl linkage).
#     - mimalloc is a vendored allocator compiled into the crate.
#   The only remaining dynamic deps on Linux are the C runtime (glibc/libpthread)
#   and on macOS the system dylibs (libSystem etc.) — both categories are always
#   present on any deploy host and do not need to appear in the Nix closure.
#
#   snowydeer's transition_to_static sets the Buck2 link_style constraint to
#   static_pic. This statically links the Rust crate graph and any bundled C
#   deps; the OS-provided C runtime remains dynamic (glibc on Linux, libSystem
#   on Darwin). The resulting binary is "mostly static" — correct for this dep
#   profile. snowydeer's ref_scan will find zero Nix-store hash fragments in the
#   binary, so the store path will be registered with an empty reference set,
#   which is accurate: the only runtime deps are glibc/libSystem, which are not
#   in the Nix store on a NixOS deployment host either (they come from the host
#   profile, not the Nix store closure).
#
#   On Darwin, the OS does not support full static linking; transition_to_static
#   still works (it sets link_style = static_pic which rustc interprets as
#   "statically link Rust + bundled C, keep system dylibs dynamic").
#
# CI invocation:
#   buck2 bxl //snowydeer:snowydeer.bxl:main -- --target //workspace/server:nudox_pkg
# Output: /nix/store/<hash>-nudox_pkg  (content-addressed, printed to stdout)
snowydeer_package(
    name = "nudox_pkg",
    binaries = [":server"],
    visibility = ["PUBLIC"],
)